<div class="prose max-w-prose">

Tarves Heritage Centre had an aging website that had not been meaningfully
updated in years. After it was compromised and much of the content was lost, the
site was left in a vulnerable state and no longer fit for purpose. In a short
window, I recovered the content using the Internet Archive, rebuilt the site,
and extended the existing brand into a cleaner, more usable digital experience.

## The challenge

The original website was difficult for staff to maintain and expensive to keep
running. Each update required outside technical support, which made even small
content changes frustrating and slow. The site also needed to be modernised to
be easier to navigate, more mobile-friendly, and far more resilient.

## Recovery and rebuild

Using archived content as the foundation, I recreated the missing structure and
content, then redesigned the site around a clearer information hierarchy. The
new website was built to feel more welcoming and accessible while preserving the
heritage character of the organisation.

A refreshed logo and revised colour palette were introduced to strengthen the
existing identity without losing its connection to the local heritage and
community context.

## Making it editable for the client

A key part of the project was making the website simple enough for volunteers
and staff to manage themselves. The new site was structured so content could be
updated without specialist technical knowledge, which reduced ongoing costs and
made the organisation far more independent.

This meant the rebuild was not just a design refresh — it was a practical,
long-term content solution designed to be maintained by the people running the
centre.

## Result

The new website improved navigation, made content easier to find, and created
a more sustainable editing workflow for the client. It also reduced ongoing
running costs significantly while giving the heritage centre a stronger digital
presence.

You can explore the live site here: [Tarves Heritage Centre](https://www.tarvesheritage.org.uk)

